%S A399457 1,2,6,15,35,14,22,33,21,70,26,39,51,34,10,55,77,42,30,65,91,119,85,
%T A399457 95,38,46,69,57,133,154,58,87,93,62,74,111,105,110,82,123,129,86,94,
%U A399457 141,159,106,118,177,165,115,161,182,66,183,305,130,78,201,335,145,174,102,187,143,195,114
%N A399457 a(1)=1, a(2)=2; thereafter a(n) is the least unused squarefree positive integer that has a common factor with a(n-1), is coprime to a(n-2), and has a prime factor not dividing a(n-1).
%C A399457 Equivalently, this is the lexicographically earliest infinite sequence of distinct squarefree positive integers such that, for n > 2, a(n) has a common factor with a(n-1) but is coprime to a(n-2).
%C A399457 The condition that a(n) have a prime factor not dividing a(n-1) is exactly the condition needed for infinite continuation. If every prime divisor of a(n) also divided a(n-1), then no possible a(n+1) could both have a common factor with a(n) and be coprime to a(n-1).
%C A399457 The sequence is infinite. Indeed, suppose a(n-2) and a(n-1) have already been chosen. Choose a prime p dividing a(n-1). Since a(n-2) and a(n-1) are coprime, p does not divide a(n-2). Now choose a sufficiently large prime q which divides neither a(n-1) nor a(n-2). Then p*q is squarefree, is coprime to a(n-2), shares the factor p with a(n-1), introduces the new prime q, and can be chosen large enough that p*q has not occurred previously. Thus a legal unused candidate always exists.
%C A399457 Except for the initial terms 1 and 2, every term has at least two distinct prime factors.
%C A399457 This is the squarefree analog of the Enots Wolley sequence A336957.
%e A399457 a(3) = 6, since 6 is the least unused squarefree integer which shares a factor with 2, is coprime to 1, and introduces a new prime factor.
%e A399457 a(4) = 15: it shares the prime 3 with 6, is coprime to 2, and introduces the prime 5.
%e A399457 The first departure from A336957 is at n=7. There A336957 has 12, which is not squarefree, whereas here a(7)=22. The number 22 shares the factor 2 with a(6)=14, is coprime to a(5)=35, and introduces the prime 11.
%Y A399457 Cf. A336957, A338833.
%K A399457 nonn,new
%O A399457 1,2
